ng to do in addition to knowing what  the exam paper will look like it's  incredibly important to know what will  be on the exam paper I don't mean  knowing exactly what's gonna be in each  question I mean knowing what's on the  syllabus and what you can and cannot be  assessed on syllabuses are available  freely online for every example make  sure you find the right example for each  question for each exam that you're  taking but make sure you are aware of  what's expected of you and further to  that make sure you're aware of how you  are assessed so find mark schemes for  each of your exams that you're going to  be taking  know what's going to get your points and  what's considered irrelevant hopefully  all of this will be trivial to you if  you've done enough passed pickle  practice like we talked about in the  last video secondly it is incredibly  important to use time management to  divide your efforts on the exam paper  effectively so to go back to the exact  example which I had a undergraduate I  knew that the 40 marks section that I  had should take about half an hour and  the 60 marks section should take about  an hour and looking at the clock I had  to be strict on myself saying right well  that's the allocated time for this  section I've got to leave it now and go  on to the next section now it's not just  as simple as dividing up the number of  marks and allocating time based on the  percentage of marks on the paper and the  percentage of time that you have on the  total exam you should also allow  yourself a buffer at the end of the exam  to go through and check your answers and  to return to questions they had to leave  earlier now what that meant for me and  about Jack was I spent 20 minutes on the  first section and 40 or 50 minutes on  the second section and then have plenty  of time at the end to go back and finish  all the questions which I haven't quite  got this is where knowing the format of  your exam paper is very very important  and you can 